1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:03,821
Welcome to the Modern Java Enterprise Handbook.

2
00:00:03,821 --> 00:00:07,043
This is chapter twenty-nine: CompletableFuture.

3
00:00:07,493 --> 00:00:10,456
The Java 5 Future could only be waited on.

4
00:00:10,456 --> 00:00:19,418
CompletableFuture makes combining, falling back and timing out operations on the future itself, with no thread blocked waiting.

5
00:00:20,318 --> 00:00:24,077
With a plain Future, you can only call get, which blocks.

6
00:00:24,077 --> 00:00:33,310
There's no way to say, when both are done, combine them, or if this fails, use a fallback, without blocking a thread and writing it by hand.

7
00:00:33,760 --> 00:00:37,286
CompletableFuture makes each of those an operation.

8
00:00:37,286 --> 00:00:46,065
Run stock and pricing lookups, combine them into an order context, time out after two seconds, and fall back if anything fails.

9
00:00:46,065 --> 00:00:48,139
No thread waits for any of it.

10
00:00:49,039 --> 00:00:57,026
A CompletableFuture is a container that's completed exactly once: with a value, an exception, or cancellation.

11
00:00:57,026 --> 00:01:00,367
Stages registered on it run when it completes.

12
00:01:00,817 --> 00:01:09,649
Each method returns a new future for that stage, so a chain is really a dependency graph, with each stage triggered by its inputs.

13
00:01:10,099 --> 00:01:11,889
The naming has a grammar.

14
00:01:11,889 --> 00:01:14,826
thenApply transforms the value, like map.

15
00:01:14,826 --> 00:01:19,554
thenCompose is for a function that returns a future, like flatMap.

16
00:01:19,554 --> 00:01:21,703
thenCombine joins two futures.

17
00:01:21,703 --> 00:01:28,507
And every stage has an Async variant, optionally with an executor, which decides where it runs.

18
00:01:28,507 --> 00:01:30,728
That turns out to matter a lot.

19
00:01:31,628 --> 00:01:33,492
Here's the standard fan-out.

20
00:01:33,492 --> 00:01:35,623
Start three lookups in parallel.

21
00:01:35,623 --> 00:01:42,216
allOf completes when they all do, but it carries no values, so read them from the original futures.

22
00:01:42,216 --> 00:01:47,543
Calling join there doesn't block, because allOf already guaranteed they're done.

23
00:01:47,993 --> 00:01:49,677
And the classic mistake.

24
00:01:49,677 --> 00:01:55,504
If the function already returns a future, thenApply gives you a future of a future.

25
00:01:55,504 --> 00:01:57,610
Use thenCompose to flatten it.

26
00:01:57,610 --> 00:02:02,734
A nested CompletableFuture in a signature is always a missed thenCompose.

27
00:02:03,634 --> 00:02:05,498
Exceptions arrive wrapped.

28
00:02:05,498 --> 00:02:12,453
Throw a VendorUnavailableException inside a stage, and the next stage sees a CompletionException.

29
00:02:12,453 --> 00:02:16,611
So code that matches on the exception type silently fails.

30
00:02:16,611 --> 00:02:19,622
Always unwrap the cause first, then match.

31
00:02:19,622 --> 00:02:21,917
join and get wrap the cause too.

32
00:02:22,367 --> 00:02:24,665
And know the three error methods.

33
00:02:24,665 --> 00:02:28,912
exceptionally runs only on failure and returns a replacement.

34
00:02:28,912 --> 00:02:33,926
handle always runs, sees both value and exception, and returns a result.

35
00:02:33,926 --> 00:02:37,269
whenComplete always runs too, but only observes.

36
00:02:37,269 --> 00:02:42,213
It can't change the outcome, so a failure keeps flowing down the chain.

37
00:02:42,213 --> 00:02:44,511
Use it for metrics, not recovery.

38
00:02:45,411 --> 00:02:46,093
Timeouts.

39
00:02:46,093 --> 00:02:49,503
orTimeout fails the future after two seconds.

40
00:02:49,503 --> 00:02:53,065
completeOnTimeout uses a default value instead.

41
00:02:53,065 --> 00:02:59,811
And anyOf returns whichever vendor answers first, typed as Object, so you'll need a cast.

42
00:03:00,261 --> 00:03:01,665
But here's the catch.

43
00:03:01,665 --> 00:03:03,871
All of these complete the future.

44
00:03:03,871 --> 00:03:05,676
None of them stop the work.

45
00:03:05,676 --> 00:03:12,696
A timed-out call keeps running, using its thread and the downstream's capacity, and so do anyOf's losers.

46
00:03:12,696 --> 00:03:20,451
Even cancel ignores its interrupt flag, because a CompletableFuture has no reference to the thread running its task.

47
00:03:20,451 --> 00:03:24,730
Structured concurrency, in chapter thirty-two, actually cancels.

48
00:03:25,630 --> 00:03:27,410
Which thread runs a stage?

49
00:03:27,410 --> 00:03:29,807
This is what most people get wrong.

50
00:03:29,807 --> 00:03:35,763
A non-async stage on an already-complete future runs right away, on the calling thread.

51
00:03:36,213 --> 00:03:41,635
If the future isn't complete yet, it runs on whichever thread completes it.

52
00:03:41,635 --> 00:03:47,563
If that's an HTTP client's callback thread, your slow stage now blocks the client.

53
00:03:48,013 --> 00:03:52,919
The Async variant without an executor runs on the common ForkJoinPool.

54
00:03:53,369 --> 00:03:57,141
And with an executor, it runs exactly where you choose.

55
00:03:57,141 --> 00:04:00,089
For anything non-trivial, pass an executor.

56
00:04:00,989 --> 00:04:05,552
Without an executor argument, supplyAsync runs on the common pool.

57
00:04:05,552 --> 00:04:12,050
It's shared by the whole JVM, including parallel streams, and has roughly one thread per core.

58
00:04:12,050 --> 00:04:15,161
Blocking I/O there ties up those few threads.

59
00:04:15,161 --> 00:04:20,553
Enough concurrent calls exhaust the pool, and everything else using it stalls.

60
00:04:20,553 --> 00:04:24,148
So always pass an executor for anything that blocks.

61
00:04:24,148 --> 00:04:28,296
On Java 21, a virtual-thread executor is the natural choice.

62
00:04:28,746 --> 00:04:35,097
And one place CompletableFuture remains the clearest tool: adapting a callback-based SDK.

63
00:04:35,097 --> 00:04:41,162
Create an incomplete future, then complete it from the success and failure callbacks.

64
00:04:42,062 --> 00:04:47,705
Compare the same fan-out with structured concurrency, which is still a preview feature.

65
00:04:47,705 --> 00:04:51,726
Open a scope, fork three subtasks, join, and read the results.

66
00:04:52,176 --> 00:04:56,030
It reads like sequential code, with useful stack traces.

67
00:04:56,030 --> 00:04:59,403
If one subtask fails, the siblings are cancelled.

68
00:04:59,403 --> 00:05:03,602
Those are exactly the things CompletableFuture is weakest at.

69
00:05:03,602 --> 00:05:10,416
The trade-off is that it blocks the calling thread, which on a virtual thread costs almost nothing.

70
00:05:11,316 --> 00:05:12,579
Production rules.

71
00:05:12,579 --> 00:05:15,700
Always pass an executor for blocking work.

72
00:05:15,700 --> 00:05:19,489
Use thenCompose when the function returns a future.

73
00:05:19,489 --> 00:05:22,684
Unwrap CompletionException before matching.

74
00:05:22,684 --> 00:05:24,838
Put timeouts on remote calls.

75
00:05:24,838 --> 00:05:28,553
And use CompletableFuture to bridge callback APIs.

76
00:05:29,003 --> 00:05:32,083
Never run blocking I/O on the common pool.

77
00:05:32,083 --> 00:05:36,777
Never expect cancel to interrupt, or a timeout to stop the work.

78
00:05:36,777 --> 00:05:39,637
Never try to recover with whenComplete.

79
00:05:39,637 --> 00:05:45,430
And never put slow non-async stages where they'll run on someone else's thread.

80
00:05:46,330 --> 00:05:46,985
To recap.

81
00:05:46,985 --> 00:05:51,423
CompletableFuture composes async work with no thread waiting.

82
00:05:51,423 --> 00:05:54,697
thenApply is map, and thenCompose is flatMap.

83
00:05:54,697 --> 00:05:57,753
Exceptions arrive wrapped, so unwrap them.

84
00:05:57,753 --> 00:06:01,463
Timeouts and cancel don't stop the underlying work.

85
00:06:01,463 --> 00:06:05,829
And always pass an executor, never blocking the common pool.

86
00:06:06,279 --> 00:06:09,272
Next is chapter thirty: concurrent collections.

87
00:06:09,272 --> 00:06:10,545
Thanks for watching.
